Ink 1, ShapeI think that the most shocking aspect of this scene for Shakespeare\rquote s audience when the play was first performed in 1611 would have been the way in which the mariners \f1\endash  mere underlings \endash  address the aristocratic characters on the ship. \par
\par
                          Alonso. \'85Where's the master? Play the men. \par
\par
                                Boatswain.\f0\u8239?I pray now, keep below. \par
\par
                                Antonio.\u8239?Where is the master, boatswain? \par
\par
                                Boatswain.\u8239?Do you not hear him? You mar our labour: keep your              cabins: you do assist the storm. \par
\par
                            (The Tempest, I.i.6-9) \par
\par
Exchanges like this would, I think, have demonstrated to the audience the extent to which the first scene of the play turns the world upside down: amidst the storm, the rules that are normally taken to govern early modern social relations are set aside \f1\endash  courtly conventions and the established social hierarchy give way before the fact that the mariners have an expertise that the Italian aristocrats lack. This means that the Great Chain of Being is effectively subverted and undermined. In extremis, self-preservation seems to be the only principle that matters \endash  this is beautifully captured in the words exchanged between Gonzalo, the \ldblquote honest old councillor\rdblquote , and the Boatswain: \par
\par
 \par
\par
                                    Gonzalo.\u8239?\'85remember whom thou hast aboard. \par
\par
                                    Boatswain.\f0\u8239?None that I more love than myself.   \par
\par
                              (The Tempest, I.i.12-13) \par
\par
 \par
\par
It is important, then, that the actors playing the aristocrats and their courtiers express outrage amidst their terror at their predicament in this scene. Antonio and Sebastian railing against the mariners reveals their cynical, grievance-filled attitude to the world (a fact testified to by the fact that curses and insults seem their natural mode), but it is also a function of their surprise that the social rules that they had assumed would govern behaviour on the ship prove so fragile \f1\endash  it would be helpful if this idea was conveyed in performance through a genuine sense of astonishment on the part of those who consider themselves socially superior and who are, suddenly, not being obeyed by their social inferiors in the tempest.     \par
\par
 \par
\par
The Tempest is a play that concerns itself with the nature of theatrical artifice and it is apt, therefore, that all is not as it seems in the play\rquote s first, dramatic scene. The storm that we - and the characters involved \endash  take so seriously is, in fact, mere artifice \endash  a scene in a play crafted by Prospero. (This meta-theatrical element in the play is taken particularly seriously in Peter Greenaway\rquote s film version of the play, Prospero\rquote s Books, where the identification between Prospero and Shakespeare underpins the whole production.) Prospero assures his daughter, Miranda, in the next scene that \ldblquote There\rquote s no harm done\rdblquote  (I.ii.15). So the high-stakes, heart-in-mouth scene that we witness on stage is profoundly misleading. The desperate words of the characters convey a sense of their lives being at stake \endash  significantly, the majority of lines in this scene seem to be imperatives (the Boatswain\rquote s \ldblquote\'85Lay her a-hold, a-hold! set her two courses off to sea again; lay her off\'85\rdblquote  is a characteristic utterance) \endash  but in actual fact the Prospero-inspired storm is \endash  like dramatic performance itself \endash  a mere passing show.    \par
\par
 \par
\par
Yet Shakespeare does everything he possibly can in this scene to ensure that the audience invests emotionally in the action. While the early modern theatre would have lacked the technical means of the modern stage, the situation depicted would have had the highest conceivable stakes for Shakespeare\rquote s audience. Sea travel would have been more prevalent and much more hazardous in the 17th century and the world of early modern publishing was full of pamphlets like that which inspired the play (the account of the shipwreck written by William Strachey in 1610 is thought to be the primary source for the play). Shakespeare\rquote s use of the language of navigation and seamanship (\ldblquote topsail\rdblquote , \ldblquote topmast\rdblquote , \ldblquote main course\rdblquote , \ldblquote lay her off\rdblquote ) is drawn directly from contemporary pamphlets and every available linguistic and dramatic resource is made to make the audience feel the quality of the storm: characters speak across one another: the imagery of water dominates (\ldblquote these roarers\rdblquote ; \ldblquote the washing of ten tides\rdblquote ; \ldblquote every drop of water\rdblquote ); and the sense of impending disaster is always uppermost in the audience\rquote s minds (\ldblquote We split, we split!\emdash Farewell, my wife and children!\emdash /Farewell, brother!\emdash We split, we split, we split!\rdblquote ). Ideally, we should watch the first scene in the same way that Miranda watches it: she reveals in the next scene that she has  \par
\par
                                                                  \'85suffered \par
\par
                With those that I saw suffer! [seeing] A brave vessel, \par
\par
                Who had no doubt some noble creature in her, \par
\par
                Dashed all to pieces\'85    \par
\par
                (The Tempest, I.ii.5-8) \par
\par
 \par
\par
While the audience might be a little more sceptical than Miranda regarding the nobility of those on board the boat, we should watch this short, highly dramatic scene that throws us in medias res into the world of The Tempest exactly as Miranda does \endash  rapt, totally engaged, astonished at the power of nature that is revealed, in actual fact, to be the power of theatrical artifice.  \par
